---
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

interface Props {
  h1: string;
}

const { h1 } = Astro.props;
---
  <h1>{h1}</h1>

<h2 id="resumen">Summary <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> </h2>

<p>
  Website that harvests, normalizes and updates information on Argentine scientific journals
  from different bibliographic repositories automatically, and builds a unified listing
  of all the information collected. 
</p>

<p>
  <strong>GitHub repository <i class="fab fa-github"></i>: </strong>
  <a href="https://github.com/Franco2900/IARCA" target="_blank">IARCA</a>
</p>

<h2 id="tecnologias">Technologies used <i class="bi bi-laptop"></i> </h2>

<div class="row">

  <div class="col-4">
    Backend: <br>
    NodeJS <i class="fab fa-node-js"></i> with the Express framework 
  </div>

  <div class="col-4">
    Database: <br>
    SQL <i class="fas fa-database"></i>
  </div>
  
  <div class="col-4">
    Frontend: <br>
    Bootstrap <i class="fab fa-bootstrap"></i> and EJS
  </div>
  
</div>
<br>

<h2 id="descripcion">Full project description <i class="fas fa-align-left"></i> </h2>

<p>
  This is the project I built as my final thesis for my B.Sc. in Computer Systems at the
  National University of Lanús. Its name, IARCA, stands for Automated Index of Argentine
  Scientific Journals (Índice Automatizado de Revistas Científicas Argentinas).
  <br />

  <h5>Origin of the problem</h5>
  Thanks to new computing technologies, the 1990s saw
  a boom in the creation of scientific journals. Digitization made editing
  and distributing these publications easier, while at the same time significantly reducing production and maintenance costs.
  <br><br />

  <div style="text-align: center;">
    <strong>Number of new Argentine scientific journals per decade</strong>
  </div>

  <img
  src={url("images/proyectos/tesis 1.webp")}
  alt="Thesis image 1"
  class="img-uniform border border-dark border-1"
  style="cursor: pointer;"
  onclick="mostrarImagenEnNuevaVentana(this)" width="966" height="402" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
  />
  
  Source: Políticas de promoción del conocimiento y derechos de propiedad intelectual - Centro 
  Interdisciplinario de Estudios en Ciencia, Tecnología e Innovación (2023)
  <br>

  &emsp;&emsp; New technologies also bring new challenges. In the case of scientific
  journals, the main problem is interoperability between systems, due to the lack of a standard
  on which metadata to use. Each bibliographic repository asks for different metadata and enforces different rules.
  <br />
  
  &emsp;&emsp; This problem leaves Argentine scientific journals scattered across 
  multiple repositories, each with its own criteria. As a result, it is not possible to view all the 
  publications in a single place. This causes simple problems, such as not knowing the total number that exists, and also more serious difficulties, such as the inability to correctly assess editorial quality.
  <br><br />

  <h5>Goal</h5>
  The goal was to develop a program that harvests and normalizes data from Argentine scientific 
  journals to display it in a single repository. Although similar work already exists,
  such as the CONICET <a href="https://www.caicyt-conicet.gov.ar/malena/">Malena</a> database,
  built by selected experts, the IARCA project differs mainly in that the whole process
  runs automatically, without human intervention.
  <br />

  &emsp;&emsp; To achieve this, we defined which fields to harvest, which databases to collect from, and applied web scraping techniques and API usage.
  <br><br />

  <div style="text-align: center;">
    <strong>Harvested databases</strong>
  </div>
  <img
  src={url("images/proyectos/tesis 6.webp")}
  alt="Thesis image 6"
  class="img-uniform border border-dark border-1"
  style="cursor: pointer;"
  onclick="mostrarImagenEnNuevaVentana(this)" width="1200" height="562" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
  />
  <br/>

  <h5>Other features</h5>
  Besides harvesting and normalizing the information, the user is given a
  way to check for themselves whether the data was collected correctly. For each harvested scientific
  journal, the URL of the source site is provided alongside its data. Clicking the URL takes the user straight to the source.
  <br>
  
  &emsp;&emsp; 
  Since data from thousands of scientific journals was gathered, we added different filters so a user 
  can search for one specific journal (by title, print ISSN, electronic ISSN or institution). 
  <br>

  &emsp;&emsp;
  The user can download all the data from a specific repository or from the final listing in 
  .csv or .json format. We researched the legality and ethics of web scraping to determine whether it was 
  possible to publish the harvested data, and concluded that there is no issue with the user downloading it, since it comes from public bibliographic repositories and causes no economic harm.
  (Source: Tutorial: Legality and Ethics of Web Scraping - Murray State University). 
  <br>

  &emsp;&emsp;
  Finally, the thesis was deployed so it can be viewed online. It is available at the following link: <a href="https://isco.unla.edu.ar/iarca" target="_blank">IARCA</a>. 
  <br />

</p>


